 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Infinite PSNR:** If MSE is 0, PSNR is infinite. Implementations must handle this by capping the value, using a small epsilon, or flagging it, as infinite values disrupt aggregation [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Alpha Channels:** Ground truth images often contain alpha channels (transparency), while NeRFs typically render RGB. A shared preprocessing step is compositing the ground truth RGBA image with a white background to ensure fair comparison with the rendered RGB image [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Shape Mismatch:** Rendered and ground truth images must have identical shapes. Mismatches should be skipped or handled via resizing/cropping [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Infinite PSNR**: If MSE is 0, PSNR is infinite. Implementations must cap this value or use a small epsilon to prevent aggregation errors [Parent].
+*   **Alpha Channels**: GT images may have alpha channels while NeRFs render RGB. GT must be composited with a white background before comparison [Parent].
+*   **Shape Mismatch**: Rendered and GT images must have identical dimensions. Checks should be performed before metric computation [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Non-finite Values**: NaN or Inf values in metrics should be filtered out before averaging [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [LPIPS Evaluation Protocol](4376-lpips-evaluation-protocol.md)
-LPIPS is a perceptual similarity metric used in volume rendering evaluation to quantify reconstruction fidelity. It differs from pixel-wise metrics by requiring a specific input intensity scaling step before feature comparison. It is chosen when perceptual quality is more important than pixel-perfect accuracy. Link: [LPIPS Evaluation Protocol](4376-lpips-evaluation-protocol.md).
-
-### [Metric Computation (PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS)](9928-metric-computation-psnr-ssim-lpips.md)
-This method computes standard photorealistic metrics—Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR), Structural Similarity Index (SSIM), and Learned Perceptual Image Patch Similarity (LPIPS)—to quantify the fidelity of rendered images against ground truth references. It addresses the problem of evaluating novel-view synthesis quality by balancing pixel-level precision (PSNR), structural integrity (SSIM), and perceptual similarity (LPIPS). What sets this apart from raw error metrics is the inclusion of perceptual models (LPIPS) and structural models (SSIM) that correlate better with human judgment than simple pixel differences, while remaining computationally efficient enough for per-view evaluation during training loops. Link: [Metric Computation (PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS)](9928-metric-computation-psnr-ssim-lpips.md).
+LPIPS is a perceptual similarity metric used in volume rendering evaluation to quantify reconstruction fidelity. It differs from pixel-wise metrics by requiring a specific input intensity scaling step before feature comparison. It is chosen when perceptual quality is more important than pixel-wise accuracy. See [LPIPS Evaluation Protocol](4376-lpips-evaluation-protocol.md).
+
+### [Quantitative Evaluation Metrics](9928-quantitative-evaluation-metrics.md)
+Quantitative Evaluation Metrics provide a standardized numerical assessment of rendered image quality and geometric fidelity by comparing synthesized views against ground-truth data. While Unified Metric Aggregation handles the statistical aggregation of these scores, this method defines the specific computations for pixel-wise, perceptual, and distributional comparisons. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [LPIPS Evaluation Protocol](4376-lpips-evaluation-protocol.md) by encompassing the full suite of standard photometric metrics (PSNR, SSIM) alongside perceptual ones, rather than focusing exclusively on learned feature distances. See [Quantitative Evaluation Metrics](9928-quantitative-evaluation-metrics.md).
 
 ### Evaluation Reporting
-This variant extends metric aggregation to include system-level performance metrics. It reports PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS alongside the size of the resulting splats (in MB) and rendering speed on various platforms (standalone PC, PC-VR, mobile VR) [S4]. It is chosen when evaluating the practical deployability of a method in real-time applications like VR.
-
-### Quantitative Evaluation Metrics
-This variant includes temporal metrics for dynamic scenes. It reports PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS, as well as temporal Optical Flow (tOF) and temporal Perceptual Loss (tLP) [S5]. tOF examines errors in motion of the reconstructed video by comparing optical flow to ground truth (lower is better), and tLP measures perceptual distance as the change in LPIPS between consecutive frames (lower is better) [S5]. It is chosen for evaluating dynamic scene reconstruction and video consistency.
+This variant extends metric aggregation to include system-level performance metrics. It reports PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS alongside the size of the resulting model (e.g., splats in MB) and rendering speed on specific hardware platforms (standalone PC, PC-VR, mobile VR) [S4]. It is chosen when evaluating real-time applicability and memory efficiency in addition to visual fidelity.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of frames $N$ and the number of metrics $M$. Each frame requires $O(H \cdot W)$ for pixel-wise metrics (PSNR, L1) and $O(H \cdot W \cdot C)$ for perceptual metrics (LPIPS) depending on the network depth.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot M)$ to store per-view metrics if saving per-view JSON, or $O(M)$ if only global averages are kept.
-*   **Measured Performance:** On KITTI reconstruction, NSG achieves PSNR 26.66, SSIM 0.806, LPIPS 0.186, tOF 0.765×10^6, tLP 0.246×100 [S5]. On KITTI novel composition, NSG achieves PSNR 25.11, SSIM 0.789, LPIPS 0.204 [S5].
-*   **Strengths:** Provides a comprehensive view of quality (pixel, structure, perception); supports both static and dynamic scenes; integrates easily with logging frameworks (W&B).
-*   **Limitations:** Computationally expensive for LPIPS and SSIM; sensitive to image alignment and normalization; infinite PSNR requires special handling.
-*   **When to use:** Standard evaluation for novel view synthesis; comparing different NeRF/Gaussian Splatting variants; reporting results for publications.
-*   **When not to use:** Real-time inference where latency is critical; scenes with significant misalignment between render and GT.
+*   **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of valid frames $M$ and the number of metrics $K$. $O(M \cdot K \cdot H \cdot W)$ for pixel-wise metrics (PSNR, L1, SSIM). LPIPS adds overhead due to neural network feature extraction.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(M \cdot K)$ to store per-view metric lists. JSON output size is $O(M \cdot K)$.
+*   **Performance**: Depends heavily on the cost of SSIM and LPIPS. PSNR and L1 are very fast. LPIPS requires loading a pre-trained network (AlexNet or VGG) and can be slow if not batched or cached [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths**: Provides a comprehensive view of reconstruction quality across multiple dimensions (photometric, perceptual). Standardized output formats (JSON) facilitate comparison across methods.
+*   **Limitations**: Does not evaluate geometric fidelity (e.g., depth error) unless explicitly added. LPIPS is computationally expensive.
+*   **When to use**: Standard evaluation of novel view synthesis results.
+*   **When not to use**: When only specific metrics (e.g., only PSNR) are required, or when real-time performance metrics are the primary focus (use Evaluation Reporting variant).
 
 ## Background
-Unified Metric Aggregation is a standard procedure in the evaluation of NeRF and Gaussian Splatting methods. It originated from the need to standardize reporting across different volume rendering papers. Key papers include "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" [S5] and "SplaTAM: Simultaneous Tracking and Mapping using Gaussian Splatting" [S1, unofficial]. The method has become a de facto standard for reporting NVS quality, often supplemented by perceptual metrics like LPIPS [S1, S3, unofficial].
+Unified Metric Aggregation is a standard post-processing step in NeRF and Gaussian Splatting evaluation pipelines. It originated from the need to standardize reporting across different NeRF implementations (e.g., [S3, unofficial] TensoRF, [S1, unofficial] SplaTAM). The method aggregates scores computed by specific metric protocols to provide a single summary of model performance. It is widely used in academic papers and open-source repositories for benchmarking 3D reconstruction methods [S1, S2, S3, unofficial][S4].
 
